00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.110
PRESENTER: Good morning.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.960
The announcement
of the Nobel Prize

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.240
going to our friend
and colleague Wolfgang

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.300
Ketterle and his
colleagues comes

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.860
like a bright ray of light into
a very dark period for MIT,

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.220
and for our nation and world.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.330
This is really a
wonderful day for Wolfgang

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:40.350
and his colleagues, it's
a wonderful day for MIT,

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.860
and it certainly is a wonderful
day for physics and science

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.080
in general.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.670
We're absolutely delighted.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:52.050
I would also like to say
that this award reminds us

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.830
once again of how
incredibly important

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.860
it is for our
nation's universities

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.080
to keep their doors
and their campuses

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:05.430
open to those wonderful people
who come to the United States

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.100
from many other nations
around the world

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.620
to build the tremendous
intellectual and human

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.240
community that we have.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.930
But you're not here
today to hear from me,

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.560
you're here today to hear
from our colleague, Wolfgang

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.060
Ketterle, who's joined by
professors Dave Pritchard, Dan

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:26.900
Kleppner, and Tom Greytak.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.120
As you will hear, this has
been a tremendous team effort,

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.070
and has a long
history here at MIT

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:36.030
that has led up to this
wonderful day for Wolfgang.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.700
So let me introduce the man
of the hour, our colleague,

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.549
Professor Wolfgang Ketterle.

00:01:40.549 --> 00:01:44.541
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:53.523 --> 00:01:57.016
[CHEERING]

00:01:57.016 --> 00:02:01.008
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:17.500
KETTERLE: Thank
you all for coming.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.680
It's an overwhelming
experience, you know?

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.150
There are many emotions.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.870
You're overjoyed, you are
thrilled, you are scared,

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.530
it's surreal.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:30.569
It's only slowly that I
think reality sinks in.

00:02:30.569 --> 00:02:32.110
I mean, this is
something really big,

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:33.910
and it's a big distinction.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:37.600
And it's wonderful
to celebrate it,

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.260
and it's also wonderful
that here in this room

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.790
there are people who have
greatly contributed to that,

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:48.250
either my collaborators
or people who

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:52.510
have mentoring my career and
have greatly supported me.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:54.970
So it's a great
moment for MIT, it's

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:56.660
a great moment for
atomic physics,

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:59.450
it's a great moment
for all of us.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:06.859
And I'm really grateful and
proud for many, many people.

00:03:06.859 --> 00:03:09.150
AUDIENCE: Could you say
something about your colleagues

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.600
at Colorado?

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:12.300
KETTERLE: Yes.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.300
This prize is shared with
Eric Cornell and Carl Wieman

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.130
at the University of Colorado.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:22.475
And we know each other very
well, and since the early '90s

00:03:22.475 --> 00:03:26.070
we have been really racing
towards colder temperatures

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.260
and hunting for this
new form of matter.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.900
It has been the best
competition of my life.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.440
And it's just great to see that
there is no loser, no winner.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.080
I mean, we have both
shared the excitement

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.330
and the thrill of a
new field, and we all

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.495
got [INAUDIBLE] cognition.

00:03:41.495 --> 00:03:43.120
So they have been
wonderful colleagues,

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:46.090
and they have done
excellent and great work.

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:48.340
And it's been exciting.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.208
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] history
represented by your colleagues?

00:03:51.208 --> 00:03:57.040
KETTERLE: Yeah, actually the
field of ultra-cold atoms

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:58.060
has a long history.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:01.900
And a lot of pioneering
work was done here at MIT.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:07.300
It started in the late 1970s
with Tom Greytak and Dan

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:11.680
Kleppner pursuing Bose-Einstein
condensation atomic hydrogen.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.600
And it seemed that atomic
hydrogen was really

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.570
just unique, and no other atom
would be suitable for that.

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:19.190
But then came other
people, among them

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:22.960
Dave Pritchard, who
pioneered laser cooling.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:24.910
And that opened a
new perspective.

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:26.870
And eventually,
it was the merger

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:30.490
of both fields which led
to the big breakthrough.

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.760
And I really feel privileged
that, when I joined MIT

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:37.420
in the early '90s, that I
was privileged to complete

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.780
this work and accomplish
the observation

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.580
of Bose-Einstein condensation.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.210
But MIT has been really special.

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:46.810
It's a 20-year-long
tradition of atomic physics

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:48.970
in pursuit of
Bose-Einstein condensation

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.170
which came to fruition.

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:53.720
And it's sometimes embarrassing
to see that I get recognized,

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:57.200
but I think it's a
recognition for all of us.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.707
And I'm proud to be part of MIT.

00:05:01.707 --> 00:05:02.790
AUDIENCE: One other thing.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.460
Could you talk just a
bit about explaining

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.219
Bose-Einstein condensate to
those of us who [INAUDIBLE]??

00:05:08.219 --> 00:05:08.760
KETTERLE: OK.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.020
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.480
So, well, Bose-Einstein
condensation

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.280
is a new form of matter.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.080
It's a special form
of matter which

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.690
forms at the lowest
temperatures ever achieved.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.850
But just to address
you as lay people,

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.790
you may wonder why can
get physicists so excited

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:26.180
about a little puff
of gas in millimeter

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:30.230
or sub-millimeter-sized
cloud of almost nothing?

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:34.570
But we get excited because
it has special properties.

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.570
The sample consists
of millions of atoms,

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:39.730
and they all
oscillate in lockstep.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.390
They sing in unison.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:43.780
It's a very special
form of matter, which

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.910
has very special properties.

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:49.330
And by discovering and
studying this form of matter,

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:51.340
we learn more about nature.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.770
And I think this is the first
[INAUDIBLE] for physicists

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:56.950
if you discover a
new form of matter,

00:05:56.950 --> 00:06:00.160
if you explore new properties.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:03.040
You have a deep look into
the beauty of nature.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:05.380
And this is exciting.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:09.310
Now, this form of matter,
because it is so special,

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:11.410
has widespread implications.

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:15.280
And some of them may
be in the applied area.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.830
So there is speculation
that this form of matter

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:20.590
may find uses for
precision measurements,

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:24.490
for better atomic clocks,
for nanotechnology, maybe

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:28.340
even for a novel form
of quantum computation.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:34.520
But this is the job of my
team and the other groups

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:38.480
worldwide to explore that and
figure out if that is possible.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:40.640
But it is typical for
fundamental research

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:43.130
that there is something
which has a new quality,

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:45.650
and then there are
numerous possibilities

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:48.460
which can be explored.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:50.320
Maybe to give you
an analogy, what

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.760
has been accomplished with
Bose-Einstein condensation

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.770
is for atoms for
material particles

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:59.530
the same step which was
taken for light when

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:01.000
the laser was invented.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.070
You all know the difference
between a light bulb

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:04.780
and a laser beam.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:09.770
Until the discovery in '95
in atomic physics with atoms,

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:11.680
we were in the era
of light bulbs.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:15.550
We only had atomic light
bulbs, atoms randomly moving.

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:18.710
But all of a sudden, with
a breakthrough in '95,

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:20.560
we have now streams
of atoms which

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:23.650
have exactly the same
properties as a laser.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:26.410
All atoms move in lockstep,
highly directional

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:27.980
beams of atoms.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:29.920
So in some sense, we
have repeated something

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:34.630
with atoms what was accomplished
with the optical laser some 30

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:36.280
years earlier.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:39.640
And I think it's fair to say
that those developments have

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:42.110
revolutionized atomic physics.

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:46.668
It has changed the way how
atomic physics is done now.

00:07:46.668 --> 00:07:49.003
MODERATOR: Questions?

00:07:49.003 --> 00:07:51.350
AUDIENCE: As a physicist,
is it every physicist's goal

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:53.146
to win the Nobel like it
would be to win the Super

00:07:53.146 --> 00:07:54.286
Bowl for a football player?

00:07:54.286 --> 00:07:55.440
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:56.860
KETTERLE: No, definitely not.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:58.940
We do physics for
the excitement,

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:01.490
we do physics for
just being creative.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:05.400
And of course, we pick our
research areas to have impact.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:09.430
But the Nobel Prize is something
which is so extraordinary,

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:11.060
we are not working for that.

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:15.750
This is sort of something which,
yeah, for just a few of us

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:17.500
may come as icing on the cake.

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:20.310
But we get our excitement
and our satisfaction

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.875
by doing good physics.

00:08:22.875 --> 00:08:24.166
AUDIENCE: I have two questions.

00:08:24.166 --> 00:08:27.568
One is that you made this
discovery of this new kind

00:08:27.568 --> 00:08:29.512
of matter in 1995.

00:08:29.512 --> 00:08:33.400
Was there something that
happened between '95 and now,

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:35.344
or were you awarded
this recognition

00:08:35.344 --> 00:08:38.780
from what happened in 1995?

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:40.970
KETTERLE: I think the
breakthrough was in '95.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:42.409
It was a new form of matter.

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:46.079
But its impact could not
be predicted at that point.

00:08:46.079 --> 00:08:47.870
And what has happened
in the last six years

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:52.010
is that numerous research
groups and my own research group

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:54.200
have exploited this
new form of matter,

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:57.560
and have shown that it has many
special properties, that it

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.950
can be used for many
fundamental studies in physics.

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:03.680
And people have also
taken first steps

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:06.950
to build matter wave sensors,
new sensors with that.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:11.670
So the full impact, the
breadth of this discovery,

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:14.085
it took a few years for
that to become visible.

00:09:14.085 --> 00:09:16.293
AUDIENCE: When you talk
about nanotechnology and some

00:09:16.293 --> 00:09:22.750
of the applications, could you
give us a more solid example

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:26.178
of what you think in the
future will be possible,

00:09:26.178 --> 00:09:30.170
something that maybe would
be used by a consumer?

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:33.663
I'm not really sure, but
or in the medical field,

00:09:33.663 --> 00:09:37.170
or is there something like that?

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:41.250
KETTERLE: I think every
specific prediction

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:42.820
will be proven false.

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:43.320
And--

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:44.671
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:44.671 --> 00:09:46.420
--that has actually
happened to the field.

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:49.380
People didn't predict what has
happened in the last few years.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:53.640
But I don't want to
dodge the question,

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.740
but let me first be general.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:58.560
We have atoms now with a
quality of laser light.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:01.710
Atoms are the basic
building blocks of nature,

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:05.940
and we now have unprecedented
control over those building

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:06.930
blocks of nature.

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:08.370
This must be good for anything.

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:11.520
This must be good for many
things we can't even think of.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:13.920
And what people
are discussing is

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:16.470
that you might now use
atoms and deposit them

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:21.030
on surfaces in fine patterns
and create some nano devices

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:22.470
of the future.

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:25.390
Other people think
that those atoms which

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:30.185
oscillate in lockstep
may eventually

00:10:30.185 --> 00:10:33.840
be the clockwork
of novel computers.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.410
But I want to stay
vague because these are

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:41.160
areas which are in exploration.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:44.410
And the future will tell
what comes out of it.

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:46.350
But these are the
buzzwords, nanotechnology,

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:50.160
new forms of computation,
new atomic clocks.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:52.538
And that's what
groups are working on.

00:10:52.538 --> 00:10:54.662
KASTNER: There may be
questions from the telephone.

00:10:54.662 --> 00:10:57.122
You could ask if there are
questions from the telephone.

00:10:57.122 --> 00:10:59.234
Just ask if [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:59.234 --> 00:11:01.150
Ask if there are questions
from the telephone.

00:11:01.150 --> 00:11:04.199
KETTERLE: Are there
questions from the telephone?

00:11:04.199 --> 00:11:05.240
AUDIENCE: They're typing.

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:06.720
AUDIENCE: This is
Robert Lee [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:08.010
from the Los Angeles Times.

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:10.050
I wonder, sir, if you
would expand a bit

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:15.350
on the specific unusual
properties of the condensate

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:17.790
that you are today
being honored for.

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:20.570
I know you talked about
the moving in lockstep,

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:23.460
but I know that you've spent
considerable time examining

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:24.502
their broader properties.

00:11:24.502 --> 00:11:26.668
And I wonder if you could
tell us a little bit about

00:11:26.668 --> 00:11:27.550
that, please.

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:30.080
KETTERLE: Yes.

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:32.785
The Bose-Einstein
condensate has some cousins.

00:11:32.785 --> 00:11:36.830
And those are superfluids
and superconductors.

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:39.230
You may be most familiar
with superconductors,

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:42.670
where electrical currents can
flow without any dissipation.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:45.010
A superconducting
wire does not heat up

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:47.230
when carbon flows through that.

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:50.890
And the Bose-Einstein condensate
has similar properties

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:54.280
that atoms can be in
motion without friction,

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:56.720
without slowing down.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:58.750
So those properties--
superconductivity,

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:02.040
superfluidity, they
carry the word "super"

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:04.000
because there's
something special to it.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:10.240
And the Bose-Einstein condensate
is the most fundamental example

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:14.890
in this family of
super states of matter.

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:18.542
So it's coherence, absence
of friction, superfluidity.

00:12:18.542 --> 00:12:20.250
These are more technical
words, but these

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:23.040
are the buzzwords
which I would associate

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:26.790
with very special properties.

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:29.270
AUDIENCE: This is [INAUDIBLE]
from the Washington Post.

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:33.734
Could you discuss your
atomic laser a little bit,

00:12:33.734 --> 00:12:38.694
and tell me how you
were able to direct

00:12:38.694 --> 00:12:42.680
the particles at a target?

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:47.600
KETTERLE: OK, the question
is about the atom laser,

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:52.640
how we direct particles
and how we created it.

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:57.140
When the Bose condensate
was discovered in '95,

00:12:57.140 --> 00:13:00.770
people saw that it was an
ultra-cold cloud of atoms,

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:04.310
but people hadn't shown directly
that those atoms clearly

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:05.780
march in lockstep.

00:13:05.780 --> 00:13:09.620
And this is the most
important property of a laser,

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:12.200
that it is one big wave.

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:15.210
In the case of light, it's
one big electromagnetic wave.

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:18.560
In the case of atoms, it's
one giant matter wave.

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:20.870
And when we realized
the atom laser,

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:23.240
we were able to show just that.

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:26.040
We could show that the
atoms in the Bose condensate

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:27.830
are really wave-like.

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:30.990
And secondly, we could create
the first rudimentary atom

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:35.810
laser by having atoms dripping
out of the condensate.

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:38.960
They were accelerated
by gravity and formed

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:43.040
the first coherent atomic
beams, again an analogy

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:46.520
with laser beams, which are
coherent beams of light.

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:48.620
Since then, other
groups have shown

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:51.710
how you can create more
directional atom lasers, most

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:54.680
notably the group of Bill
Phillips at Gaithersburg.

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:57.420
He was a Nobel laureate for
laser cooling four years ago.

00:14:00.977 --> 00:14:02.870
AUDIENCE: I have a
few more questions.

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:05.175
What is your next step
with your research,

00:14:05.175 --> 00:14:07.835
and what are you going
to do with the money?

00:14:07.835 --> 00:14:10.709
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:13.245 --> 00:14:14.870
KETTERLE: The next
step in the research

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:20.060
is to use the Bose condensate
to test fundamental physics,

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:24.770
to realize more
different and more subtle

00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:26.350
new forms of matter.

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:28.570
We also have
experiments where we

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:32.080
try to put both
condensates on chips,

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:34.690
on micro fabricated surfaces.

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:37.060
So there is some hope
that we can create,

00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:41.620
in the future, atom chips where,
you know, in ordinary chips,

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:45.340
the information is carried
by electrons moving in wires.

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:47.350
And now we want to
create atom chips

00:14:47.350 --> 00:14:51.070
where atoms move close to a
surface and are guided around.

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:54.660
So we're talking about
a next major step,

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:57.660
and new perspectives
to the field.

00:14:59.861 --> 00:15:01.860
I haven't thought what
I'm doing with the money.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:06.150
I mean, I have a family, and
so there are many good uses.

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:10.830
But I have not spent any
second to think about it.

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:15.250
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
I have two questions.

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:16.980
First of all, where
were you when you

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:18.790
got the call winning the prize?

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:21.000
And second of all, you
touched on this a little,

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:27.450
but all of you are very
young and [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:30.640
after the research significance
has had a chance to sink in.

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:33.860
What do you think it is
about your research that

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:36.080
has made the physics
community immediately

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:38.500
recognize its importance?

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:40.680
KETTERLE: OK, the
first question is

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:44.820
where was I when I was
notified about the prize.

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:45.540
I was in the bed.

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:47.450
I was sleeping.

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:51.660
And it was 5:30 in the
morning, and the phone rang.

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:54.300
And it was Professor
[? Norbi, ?] the Secretary

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:57.750
of the Swedish Academy of
Sciences, who told me he has

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:59.370
some important news to tell me.

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:01.950
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:05.010
And then he informed me
that I am the co-recipient

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:06.250
of this year's Nobel Prize.

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:14.070
The other question was
that we are fairly young.

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:18.350
Yeah, it's probably
rather unusual

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:21.800
that research is
recognized just a few years

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:23.630
after the discovery.

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:29.040
But I think this reflects
that many colleagues worldwide

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:32.030
were immediately picking
up on those developments.

00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:37.130
And I estimate that, since
'95, 2,000 scientific research

00:16:37.130 --> 00:16:40.670
papers have been written on the
subject, which is really a lot.

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:44.270
So worldwide, the activities
of hundreds of scientists.

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:48.500
And I think this just showed
that this research had impact.

00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:51.740
It has revolutionized
atomic physics.

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:53.650
So researchers vote
with their time.

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:57.410
And many of my colleagues have
voted that this is important,

00:16:57.410 --> 00:17:00.200
and that this is promising
for further exploration

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:01.407
and developments.

00:17:08.562 --> 00:17:12.460
MODERATOR: Are there
questions from the floor?

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:16.920
Are there questions from
the telephone group?

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:18.586
AUDIENCE: I guess i
just want to clarify

00:17:18.586 --> 00:17:22.780
that one thing you're saying
about the atoms on the chips.

00:17:22.780 --> 00:17:25.625
Does that mean
possibly, as I've read--

00:17:25.625 --> 00:17:28.990
and I can't believe everything
I've read-- that you're talking

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:30.520
about the faster computers?

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:34.520
Is that what you?

00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:35.890
KETTERLE: Faster computers.

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:39.780
Well, this research is
about ultra-cold atoms.

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:42.990
And ultra-cold means that
the atoms are very slow.

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:45.090
So we won't have an
advantage in speed

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:49.560
because atoms are moving sort
of like a lightning bolt.

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:54.660
But there's some hope to
use the coherence of atoms

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:56.520
for a new way of computation.

00:17:56.520 --> 00:18:00.120
So quantum computers are
not necessarily faster

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:02.310
in terms of their clock
speed, they're just

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:03.730
much more efficient.

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:06.180
They do parallel processing.

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:08.490
It's a heavy
parallelism, which makes

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:13.720
them win big in that regard.

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:18.030
But if atom chips will lead
to this new class of quantum

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:20.040
computers remains to be seen.

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:23.340
This area of atom chips
is really in its infancy.

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:25.991
But it's a promising
direction to pursue.

00:18:25.991 --> 00:18:27.699
AUDIENCE: Professor,
can you explain just

00:18:27.699 --> 00:18:31.650
how cold [INAUDIBLE]?

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:32.340
KETTERLE: OK.

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:36.300
Bose-Einstein condensates are
characterized by temperatures

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:38.040
in the nanokelvin regime.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:40.170
This is more than a
million times colder

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:43.510
than interstellar space.

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:45.090
So sometimes, I
mean, you can say

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:47.340
why is it not cold
enough to be 1,000 times

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:49.090
colder than interstellar space.

00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:51.960
So well, there are phenomena
in physics and novel

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:55.180
properties of matter which
require to go even colder.

00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:58.650
And so it was only when we
had developed techniques

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:01.200
to cool more than
a million times

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:04.620
lower than the temperature
of interstellar space

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:07.313
that we could observe
this new form of matter.

00:19:07.313 --> 00:19:08.792
AUDIENCE: And were
the experiments

00:19:08.792 --> 00:19:13.170
actually carried out at labs
here at MIT, or in both?

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:15.480
KETTERLE: They were
carried out in both labs.

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:17.820
There were independent
efforts at Boulder

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:21.150
using rubidium atoms, at
MIT using sodium atoms.

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:23.640
We were feverishly working
on those experiments

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:25.180
for several years.

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:28.215
And it was sort of remarkable
that, within a few months,

00:19:28.215 --> 00:19:30.210
both groups succeeded.

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:32.970
It was in June that they had
the breakthrough in Boulder,

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:35.550
and in September when the
breakthrough at MIT happened.

00:19:37.902 --> 00:19:39.360
MODERATOR: I want
to add that there

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:41.700
were a number of other
Nobel Prize winners

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:43.000
here in the room to celebrate.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:46.470
And I wanted to invite them
to come up to join Wolfgang.

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:50.350
Sam Ting.

00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:51.499
Professor Sam Ting.

00:19:51.499 --> 00:19:53.540
AUDIENCE: Dr. Ketterle,
I wondered if you would--

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:55.364
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:19:55.364 --> 00:19:56.780
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
Nobel Prize.

00:19:56.780 --> 00:20:02.478
MODERATOR: Gary [? Friedman. ?]
And anyone else.

00:20:02.478 --> 00:20:04.938
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:04.938 --> 00:20:08.382
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:20:18.076 --> 00:20:19.744
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:19.744 --> 00:20:23.153
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:20:32.151 --> 00:20:33.650
MODERATOR: The left
to right on this

00:20:33.650 --> 00:20:36.526
is Ting, Ketterle,
Friedman, and Sharp.

00:20:36.526 --> 00:20:38.442
AUDIENCE: But don't stop
smiling [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:38.442 --> 00:20:41.394
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:45.929 --> 00:20:48.220
MODERATOR: And I also want
to bring in the students who

00:20:48.220 --> 00:20:51.090
worked with Wolfgang
Ketterle on this project, who

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:53.476
are waiting in the wings.

00:20:53.476 --> 00:20:57.332
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:10.110
MODERATOR: Dan is the head
of the [INAUDIBLE] lab

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:11.930
of electronics,
which has sponsored

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:13.574
much of Wolfgang's research.

00:21:13.574 --> 00:21:14.990
Would you like to
say a few words?

00:21:18.406 --> 00:21:20.274
Daniel Kleppner.

00:21:20.274 --> 00:21:22.190
KLEPPNER: I'm not the
director of [? Areli. ?]

00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:26.470
That's [? Irwin ?]
Shapiro, who is here.

00:21:26.470 --> 00:21:36.750
And it's a wonderful
day for me personally,

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:40.170
and I think for my
colleagues, because we

00:21:40.170 --> 00:21:43.290
have watched Wolfgang's
work since he came to MIT,

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:48.390
and admired so much how he
went about his work, his taste,

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:50.100
his goal, his enthusiasm.

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:54.390
He's a marvelous colleague, and
his accomplishment certainly

00:21:54.390 --> 00:21:56.406
deserves the prize.

00:21:56.406 --> 00:21:57.780
We're very pleased
that he has it

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:00.150
because there are many
accomplishments in science

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:03.160
which deserve the
prize but don't get it.

00:22:03.160 --> 00:22:05.880
And in this case, we
have a success story

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:09.960
where the work really deserves
the prize and does get it.

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:14.340
I'm particularly happy
today because I've

00:22:14.340 --> 00:22:16.560
had a long association
with the subject.

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:21.360
Tom Greytak and I many years
ago were among the first groups

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:24.420
to start searching for
Bose-Einstein condensation

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:27.330
in an atomic gas.

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:29.970
I knew nothing about it at all
before Tom explained to me what

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:32.370
Bose-Einstein condensation was.

00:22:32.370 --> 00:22:36.430
And he explained it to me, and
it sounded kind of interesting.

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:38.520
So it was really
through his stimulation

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:42.180
that we started out in
this work many years ago.

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:44.290
And eventually, we did get it.

00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:47.850
But we started very soon, and
we pursued the only method

00:22:47.850 --> 00:22:51.540
open at the time, which
was with atomic hydrogen.

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:54.900
Later, when laser cooling
was invented and created,

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:58.620
it turned out that it works much
better with the alkali metal

00:22:58.620 --> 00:22:59.520
atoms.

00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:01.080
Nonetheless, we did get it.

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:04.330
And that was a great
satisfaction to us.

00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:06.780
But I think what is even
more of a satisfaction

00:23:06.780 --> 00:23:09.630
is that this field has
grown so remarkably

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:12.870
and had such an impact
on so many scientists.

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:15.870
And I take a
personal satisfaction

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:18.150
not only because of the
work that Tom Greytak

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:20.320
and I, the early
work that we did,

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:23.850
which is a prelude to this,
but because of the people

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:26.370
involved with it.

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:30.930
The Scientific American dubbed
me the grandfather of BEC.

00:23:30.930 --> 00:23:34.715
Well, grandfathers can
be very happy people.

00:23:34.715 --> 00:23:37.390
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:37.390 --> 00:23:41.790
And I think I'm the grandfather
because Dave Pritchard was

00:23:41.790 --> 00:23:45.960
my student, and he
brought in Wolfgang

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:47.050
and mentored his career.

00:23:47.050 --> 00:23:49.170
So I'd say Dave Pritchard
then is the father

00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:51.580
[INAUDIBLE] grandfather.

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:55.010
And Tom Greytak was
not a student of mine.

00:23:55.010 --> 00:23:55.950
He's a brother.

00:23:55.950 --> 00:23:57.190
So let me [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:00.264 --> 00:24:02.430
MODERATOR: While we're
talking about family matters,

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:06.390
we have family that matters to
Wolfgang here, his two sons.

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:09.458
And I just want them to come
up, and he can introduce them.

00:24:09.458 --> 00:24:12.846
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:19.302 --> 00:24:21.010
KETTERLE: So well, I
have three children,

00:24:21.010 --> 00:24:24.150
and they are all different.

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:26.820
My daughter told me this morning
she wanted to go to school.

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:29.195
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:29.195 --> 00:24:31.607
Holger made already a memo.

00:24:31.607 --> 00:24:32.940
My daughter, she's 12 years old.

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:34.300
Holger is nine years old.

00:24:34.300 --> 00:24:36.160
And Holger, he
made a [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:36.160 --> 00:24:37.220
to one of the reporters.

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:39.060
He said, the best
thing about my dad

00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:40.530
winning the Nobel
Prize is I don't

00:24:40.530 --> 00:24:41.654
have to go to school today.

00:24:41.654 --> 00:24:44.320
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:45.510
And this is Jonas.

00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:49.230
He's 15 years old, and he's
at Brookline High School.

00:24:51.760 --> 00:24:53.650
But getting more
personal, I also

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:56.470
ask Dave Pritchard to stand
up and come next to me,

00:24:56.470 --> 00:25:01.720
because it really speaks for
how the MIT system worked.

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:04.140
He hired me as a
postdoc in 1990.

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:06.010
I was in a different
field of physics,

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:07.960
and he was in the
cold atom field.

00:25:07.960 --> 00:25:11.080
He hired me as a postdoc,
and I learned from him.

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:13.390
And after three years
of being a postdoc,

00:25:13.390 --> 00:25:18.110
he said, so well, I've
taught you many things,

00:25:18.110 --> 00:25:20.990
but now you can take my
lab and be on your own.

00:25:20.990 --> 00:25:22.820
And two years
later, we discovered

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:24.080
Bose-Einstein condensation.

00:25:24.080 --> 00:25:27.500
And it was sort of
executing a common agenda.

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:30.110
Some person on the phone
commented on my young age,

00:25:30.110 --> 00:25:36.038
but there's also the wisdom of
an older person [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:36.038 --> 00:25:39.982
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:56.290 --> 00:26:00.460
PRITCHARD: Well, you know,
when Wolfgang was my postdoc,

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:05.530
we came up with an idea that
was a modification of a trap

00:26:05.530 --> 00:26:08.980
that I'd co-invented earlier.

00:26:08.980 --> 00:26:10.660
And this idea
enabled us-- it was

00:26:10.660 --> 00:26:14.525
called a dark spot
[INAUDIBLE],, a dark spot trap,

00:26:14.525 --> 00:26:18.310
and it enabled us to hide
lots of the cold atoms

00:26:18.310 --> 00:26:21.100
in the middle, in a dark spot
where the laser light didn't

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:22.450
shine, actually.

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:26.140
And the rest of the atoms were
pushed in by more laser light.

00:26:26.140 --> 00:26:28.360
And the result of
this was that we

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:32.350
had gotten to a density of
atoms where it was apparent

00:26:32.350 --> 00:26:35.410
that if we got really the
vaccum a little bit better,

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:38.800
we could begin to get much
colder by using the technique

00:26:38.800 --> 00:26:41.620
that Harold Hess and
Dan Kleppner and Tom

00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:45.640
Greytak had developed
called evaporative cooling.

00:26:45.640 --> 00:26:46.580
That's very simple.

00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:48.940
It's just the way
you cool your coffee.

00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:52.300
You let the hottest
molecules escape,

00:26:52.300 --> 00:26:54.940
and then the ones that
are left behind are cold.

00:26:54.940 --> 00:26:57.490
Unfortunately, that doesn't
work in a very dilute gas.

00:26:57.490 --> 00:26:59.730
You need to have
lots of collisions.

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:02.140
And so people have always
come up to me and said, Dave,

00:27:02.140 --> 00:27:04.360
you know, what a sacrifice
you made by giving up

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:06.130
this experiment,
you know, just when

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:09.400
it was on the brink
of more progress.

00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:15.310
And to me, it was a really
obvious and pleasurable,

00:27:15.310 --> 00:27:18.280
and in fact this today makes it
even more pleasurable to have

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:24.594
done that, because what I got
for giving up one of my three

00:27:24.594 --> 00:27:27.010
experiments-- and the other
two then went better because I

00:27:27.010 --> 00:27:28.330
could work on them--

00:27:28.330 --> 00:27:29.830
was a wonderful colleague.

00:27:29.830 --> 00:27:31.960
I just can't explain
to you how much fun

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:35.590
it was to work with
Wolfgang then as a postdoc,

00:27:35.590 --> 00:27:38.650
and now that we're doing
some new things with making

00:27:38.650 --> 00:27:42.310
atom interferometers
on a chip and so forth.

00:27:42.310 --> 00:27:45.339
And I mean, let's face
it, the experiment

00:27:45.339 --> 00:27:47.880
went a heck of a lot better with
him running it than with me.

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:51.000
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:51.000 --> 00:27:52.860
And so I thought it
was an easy call,

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:56.220
and especially to get another
addition to the atomic physics

00:27:56.220 --> 00:27:57.420
group here.

00:27:57.420 --> 00:27:58.990
It's been worth it.

00:27:58.990 --> 00:28:00.974
And we're just so
happy he stayed.

00:28:00.974 --> 00:28:04.942
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:17.350 --> 00:28:19.080
MODERATOR: We also
do teaching at MIT,

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:21.870
and the cutting edge this
represents is part of that.

00:28:21.870 --> 00:28:23.871
I want to introduce Marc
Kastner, the department

00:28:23.871 --> 00:28:25.911
head of physics, who's
going to talk a little bit

00:28:25.911 --> 00:28:27.570
about Wolfgang's teaching.

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:30.990
KASTNER: Wolfgang is one of
our most dedicated, passionate

00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:32.490
teachers.

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:35.370
He's a superb educator
of graduate students.

00:28:35.370 --> 00:28:38.200
We're going to bring his
students up in a minute.

00:28:38.200 --> 00:28:42.300
He's a superb classroom teacher
at the undergraduate level.

00:28:42.300 --> 00:28:44.280
This summer, he even took
a high school student

00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:48.480
into his lab to excite
him about research.

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:51.720
I think he's the perfect example
of what a faculty member ought

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:53.910
to be at a research
university, someone who

00:28:53.910 --> 00:28:56.640
does cutting-edge
research and brings

00:28:56.640 --> 00:29:00.820
the excitement of that
research to the students.

00:29:00.820 --> 00:29:04.420
And so just on behalf
of the department,

00:29:04.420 --> 00:29:07.182
I want to thank you for
everything you do, Wolfgang.

00:29:07.182 --> 00:29:10.549
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:14.747 --> 00:29:16.330
KETTERLE: But I think
it's really time

00:29:16.330 --> 00:29:18.460
that all the people who
currently work with me just

00:29:18.460 --> 00:29:22.759
come up and join
this moment with me.

00:29:22.759 --> 00:29:26.210
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:44.451 --> 00:29:48.395
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:30:01.045 --> 00:30:04.960
MODERATOR: [INAUDIBLE]
Nobel scientist.

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:07.750
KETTERLE: I could introduce
everybody individually and tout

00:30:07.750 --> 00:30:11.740
his accomplishments, but
this is a fantastic team.

00:30:11.740 --> 00:30:13.630
And accomplishment
in science really

00:30:13.630 --> 00:30:15.952
require to work with a team.

00:30:15.952 --> 00:30:17.410
I mean, the people
who stand behind

00:30:17.410 --> 00:30:21.280
me also speak for this
explosive growth in the field.

00:30:21.280 --> 00:30:23.590
When the research
was initiated, it

00:30:23.590 --> 00:30:25.690
was just myself and
two graduate student,

00:30:25.690 --> 00:30:27.550
or sorry, three
graduate students.

00:30:27.550 --> 00:30:29.350
And then the team was growing.

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:32.140
Right now, we have a major
effort in different laps

00:30:32.140 --> 00:30:34.960
just to explore all
those new possibilities.

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:38.480
But I really want to give
a big hand to this team.

00:30:38.480 --> 00:30:41.333
They are really carrying
on this exciting research.

00:30:41.333 --> 00:30:44.784
[CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]

