00:31:51.590 --> 00:31:54.090
MODERATOR: Are there are any
other questions from the press,

00:31:54.090 --> 00:31:56.850
either on the phone or here?

00:31:56.850 --> 00:32:00.770
AUDIENCE: I have one question
if we have [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:00.770 --> 00:32:03.412
I wonder, sir, if,
on the occasion

00:32:03.412 --> 00:32:06.850
of the centennial
Nobel Prize, you

00:32:06.850 --> 00:32:11.880
might reflect for us on the
relationship between the Nobel

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:15.584
Prize and the pursuit of
excellence in science.

00:32:21.510 --> 00:32:23.150
KETTERLE: So well,
I mean the question

00:32:23.150 --> 00:32:26.390
is about the centennial
of the Nobel Prize.

00:32:26.390 --> 00:32:29.810
I think it's so special
to win the Nobel Prize,

00:32:29.810 --> 00:32:31.100
nothing can top it.

00:32:31.100 --> 00:32:36.020
So the fact that it's the 100th
anniversary, it makes it--

00:32:36.020 --> 00:32:37.460
does it make it
even more special?

00:32:37.460 --> 00:32:41.270
I think it's so special
that nothing can top it.

00:32:41.270 --> 00:32:44.610
But I think 100 years
is a long tradition.

00:32:44.610 --> 00:32:47.990
And I think for all
of those 100 years,

00:32:47.990 --> 00:32:51.230
the Nobel Prize has really
distinguished great science.

00:32:51.230 --> 00:32:56.930
And it shows that science
is exciting, and has not

00:32:56.930 --> 00:33:00.730
lost any of its excitement
in the past 100 years.

